{% extends "base.html.jinja2" %}
{% import "_macros.html.jinja2" as ui %}

{% block body %}
<section class="hero">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">Free and open source · {{ license_name }} · MCP server</p>
    <h1>Infrastructure provisioning for your coding agent.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      usecode is a free, open source MCP server. Point your agent at it and it
      creates servers, volumes, networks and DNS on Hetzner, AWS, Google Cloud,
      Azure and the rest — asked for in plain language, planned before applied.
      Without it, your agent writes the app and hands you a console.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="btn primary" href="/getting-started">Get started — free</a>
      <a class="btn ghost" href="{{ repo_url }}">View source</a>
    </div>
    {{ ui.code("uv tool install usecode-mcp\nclaude mcp add usecode -- uvx usecode-mcp", "sh") }}
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap section">
  <h2>Sentences that would otherwise be an afternoon</h2>
  <ul class="prompts">
    {% for prompt in prompts %}<li>“{{ prompt }}”</li>{% endfor %}
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">
    Each resolves to ordinary tool calls — plan first, then apply — against whichever
    provider holds the credentials you exported. No console tab, no ticket, no
    waiting on the one person who knows the account.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="wrap section">
  <h2>What happens without it</h2>
  <div class="grid">
    {% for feature in features %}
    <article class="card">
      <h3>{{ feature.title }}</h3>
      <p>{{ feature.body }}</p>
    </article>
    {% endfor %}
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap section">
  <h2>Providers</h2>
  <ul class="chips">
    {% for provider in providers %}<li>{{ provider }}</li>{% endfor %}
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="wrap section">
  <h2>Free and open source — the whole thing</h2>
  <p>
    Open-core tools keep the provider you actually run, or the audit trail your
    auditor asks for, on the other side of a quote. usecode is published whole
    under the {{ license_name }} — every tool, every provider, the entire source.
    The hosted service runs that same build, so a subscription buys operation and
    assurance, never access.
  </p>
  <ul class="ticks">
    {% for point in license_points %}<li>{{ point }}</li>{% endfor %}
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">
    <a href="{{ source_license_url }}">Read the licence</a> ·
    <a href="/team">Why anyone pays for it</a>
  </p>
</section>

<section class="wrap section">
  <h2>Somebody operates this. Decide who.</h2>
  <div class="grid three">
    {% for edition in editions %}{{ ui.edition_card(edition) }}{% endfor %}
  </div>
  <p class="muted">
    The paid plans add no software. They add an endpoint that stays up, a restore
    that has been rehearsed, and someone contractually on the hook — otherwise all
    three are you.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="wrap section">
  <h2>Questions</h2>
  {{ ui.faq_list(faqs) }}
</section>
{% endblock %}
